> 
> Dear Professor Rising,
> 
> I hope this message finds you well. My name is Chenxi Yan, a final-year M.E. student in Agricultural Engineering at South China Agricultural University. I am writing to express my strong interest in pursuing a PhD under your supervision at the University of Delaware. I was encouraged to reach out to you by Professor Robert Kopp at Rutgers University, with whom I recently had an excellent conversation about my doctoral aspirations of the resilence in MACH. Professor Kopp felt that my background in climate‑driven flood risk assessment, multi‑model uncertainty analysis, and multi‑objective decision‑making would resonate with your group's research on climate risk, integrated assessment, and robust adaptation strategies under deep uncertainty.
> 
> Your distinguished research at the intersection of climate science, economics, and decision analysis—particularly your work on the Framework for Assessing Changes To Sea‑level (FACTS), your leadership in the Coastal Impact Lab, and your investigations into how deep uncertainty in climate projections can be incorporated into robust adaptation planning for coastal communities—represents exactly the kind of transdisciplinary, decision‑oriented climate risk science that I have been building toward in my master's research and am determined to advance at the doctoral level. I was especially inspired by your work on developing open‑source, transparent models for sea‑level rise impact assessment and your commitment to making climate risk information accessible and actionable for decision‑makers.
> 
> My master's research has spanned both flood hazard modeling and multi‑objective decision analysis, providing a dual foundation that I believe aligns naturally with your group's approach to coupled climate risk and adaptation assessment. I led a project that developed a multi-scale flood loss assessment model that integrates crop-specific submergence-sensitivity coefficients. Using the CMIP5 ensemble under RCP4.5 and RCP8.5, I elucidated the spatio-temporal evolution of flood losses for the three major staple crops in China towards 2050 and 2080. This research required me to extract extreme precipitation indices from global climate model outputs, perform statistical analysis using Python and R, and conduct spatial analysis with ArcGIS Pro. A key manuscript from this work, " Assessing Future Agricultural Flood Risks in China Under Climate Change: Shifting Peaks and Land-Use Feedback," on which is currently under revision at Earth's Future. In a complementary project on the Leizhou Peninsula—a typhoon-prone coastal agricultural region—I confronted the limitations of traditional depth-damage curves, which ignore the effects of flood duration, velocity, and crop physiological sensitivity. My solution was to integrate a HEC-RAS hydraulic model into a Knowledge-Guided Machine Learning (KGML) framework, where physically meaningful variables—such as inundation duration, velocity, and crop phenological stage—served as input features to a machine learner. This KGML-based strategy significantly improved loss estimation accuracy while preserving physical consistency. Furthermore, in this study, I also evaluated the impact of high-standard drainage channels on field floods and the damage caused by waterlogging to crops.
> 
> On the decision analysis side, I have hands‑on experience with the kind of multi‑objective optimization and multi‑criteria decision‑making central to your group's work. In a Guangdong provincial water‑saving irrigation project, I coupled the crop growth model AquaCropOSPy with a canal network distribution model, and employed NSGA‑II multi‑objective optimization to derive Pareto‑optimal water allocation strategies that balance irrigation efficiency, energy consumption, and crop yield outcomes. To address the challenge of selecting among competing optimal solutions, I applied AHP‑Entropy‑TOPSIS multi‑criteria analysis. This experience taught me that adaptation decisions—whether about irrigation scheduling, flood protection investment, or land‑use planning—must balance multiple, often conflicting objectives under deep uncertainty, precisely the kind of decision challenge your integrated assessment frameworks are designed to address. On the infrastructure resilience side, I served as a core member of a National Natural Science Foundation of China project on urban flood resilience, where I developed a quantification framework based on system performance curves that couples hydrological‑hydrodynamic models with infrastructure network models and incorporates socioeconomic indicators to compute resilience metrics.
> 
> What draws me to your group is the opportunity to build a coherent research pipeline that moves from local physical modeling, to cross‑region transfer, to global economic impact. Aim 1: Compound Flood Modeling and Future Risk in data‑rich coastal regions. I would develop a high‑resolution compound flood model—combining storm surge, river flooding, and sea‑level rise—for the U.S. Atlantic and Gulf coasts, using hydrodynamic model coupled with probabilistic sea‑level projections under CMIP6 SSP scenarios. The goal would be to quantify joint exceedance probabilities and produce decision‑relevant flood risk maps that could directly support coastal planning and adaptation efforts. Aim 2: Transferable Compound Flood Model using Physics‑Informed Neural Operators. I would train compound flood models in data‑rich areas—such as the U.S. Atlantic and Gulf coasts, and the Greater Bay Area in China—and then use approaches like PINO to transfer these models to data‑scarce but highly vulnerable coastal deltas, such as the Mekong Delta and Manila Bay, to predict future compound flood risk there under climate scenarios. Aim 3: Compound Flood Loss Functions—bridging physical hazard and climate‑economic impact. I would build probabilistic damage functions that link compound inundation characteristics—depth, duration, and velocity—to agricultural, infrastructure, and GDP losses, ultimately extending the Climate Impact Lab framework and producing social cost of compound flooding metrics that could inform IPCC‑style assessments and adaptation finance design.
> 
> I believe this vision integrates well with your group's expertise in integrated assessment and robust decision‑making under deep uncertainty. I would be deeply honored to discuss how my background might complement your ongoing research. Thank you very much for your time and consideration. My CV, academic transcripts and a summary of my manuscript are attached for your review.
> 
> Warm regards,
> Chenxi Yan
